7 Layers of the OSI Model (Top to Bottom)

🔸 1. Physical Layer: Lowest layer in OSI model. Deals with physical connection between devices. Transmits raw binary data (1s and 0s) through cables, radio, etc.
🧰 Examples: Ethernet cables, fiber optics, switches, hubs.

🔸 2. Data Link Layer: It helps devices on the same network talk to each other. Adds MAC addresses (unique hardware ID) so your data reaches the right nearby device. Also detects and fixes basic errors.
🧰 Example: Switches, ARP, MAC address.

🔸 3. Network Layer: This layer figures out where your data should go, even across the world. Adds IP addresses to data and helps with routing through different networks.
🧰 Examples: IP, ICMP, Routers.

🔸 4. Transport Layer: Ensures reliable or fast delivery of data. Breaks data into chunks (segments) and uses TCP (reliable) or UDP (faster) to send them.
Also tracks port numbers, like doors to different apps.
🧰 Example: TCP (YouTube loading properly), UDP (live stream), port 80 (web), port 443 (secure web).

🔸 5. Session Layer: Starts, manages, and ends conversations between two devices. Think of it as a meeting room where two apps talk. Keeps track of who’s talking and when.
🧰Example: Logging in to a website and maintaining your session.
 
🔸 6. Presentation Layer: Converts data into a format that both sender and receiver understand. Deals with encryption, compression, and encoding.
🧰Example: Encrypting data with SSL/TLS, converting text into JPEG, MP4, or JSON.

🔸 7. Application Layer: Closest layer to the end-user. The layer you interact with directly (websites, apps, emails). Talks to the layers below to send/receive data on your behalf.
🧰Example: Web browser (HTTP), Email app (SMTP), File transfer (FTP), DNS, SSH.
